WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.460
The end of A Series of Unfortunate Events
is finally here and, after three seasons of

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.250
very fascinating drama, we now have answers
to many questions fans have been dying to

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:11.250
know.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:15.120
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm explaining all the mysteries

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.960
from the last season of Unfortunate Events
including the Sugar Bowl, VFD, the fate of

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.770
the Baudelaires and Count Olaf, and who Beatrice
really is.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:28.550
Keep watching to the end where I'll reveal
the final episode's secret symbolism and watch

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.600
out for the hidden VFD I've snuck into the
video.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.879
Spoilers ahead for the full series, so take
care if you're not up-to-date.

00:00:35.879 --> 00:00:39.530
One of the biggest mysteries in the series
has been the Sugar Bowl and why everyone is

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:41.820
so obsessed with getting hold of it.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:46.130
The secret is revealed in the very final episode
when Kit explains to the Baudelaires that

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.270
the bowl simply contains sugar.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:53.690
However, it's no ordinary sugar – it's a
sugar derived from a botanical hybrid that

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:57.510
VFD developed to defend against the deadly
Medusoid Mycelium.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:02.140
Which is why during the opera flashback, after
Esmé adds sugar from the sugar bowl to Kit's

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:07.360
tea, she tells her that she'll find the sugar
is extremely bitter, however, Kit never drank

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.380
the tea, sadly for her given her eventual
fate.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:15.250
Crucially, Kit also reveals to the Baudelaires
on the island that the hybrid sugar isn't

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:20.079
just a cure, it actually immunises you against
the Medusoid Mycelium.

00:01:20.079 --> 00:01:24.890
Remember when Sunny is infected with the deadly
spores from the mushroom in the Grotto, well,

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:29.750
the horseradish substitute, wasabi, that the
Baudelaires use to cure her works, but it's

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:34.840
just an antidote, not a vaccine and so Sunny
gets infected again on the island.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:40.049
In the TV series, the Medusoid Mycelium is
introduced in Season 3's The Grim Grotto episodes

00:01:40.049 --> 00:01:44.310
where it's revealed that Gregor Anwhistle
was cultivating a disease that could annihilate

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:45.810
VFD's enemies.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:50.359
The Hook-Handed Man, aka Fernald, warned him
that the weapon he was developing was too

00:01:50.359 --> 00:01:53.150
dangerous and risky as it could wipe out everyone.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:57.869
However, Gregor wouldn't listen; so, Fernald
burned down the laboratory which effectively

00:01:57.869 --> 00:02:00.390
ended up isolating the Mycelium there.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:04.909
The botanical hybrid vaccine in the sugar
bowl was recreated by the Baudelaire's parents

00:02:04.909 --> 00:02:06.899
when they were stranded on the island.

00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:10.929
The children discover this while searching
for a cure after they get infected.

00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:15.410
The solution lies in a tree their parents
grew which contains the vaccine, so when they

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:18.330
eat an apple from the tree, they're cured.

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:21.880
Interestingly, the snake that gives them the
apple in this scene is the same Incredibly

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:26.810
Deadly Viper from Uncle Montgomery's collection
that was falsely accused by Olaf of killing

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.230
Uncle Monty.

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:32.470
By the end of the series, the power of the
Sugar Bowl's contents is clear, especially

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:37.970
if a villain were to have both that and the
biological weapon that is the Medusoid Mycelium.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:42.180
The Sugar Bowl is also one of the factors
that triggers the Schism within VFD.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:47.040
In the opera flashback, Esmé and Beatrice
argue over who should keep the valuable sugar

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.480
vaccine safe.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.799
Beatrice knows there are already tensions
within the VFD organisation, with some parties

00:02:52.799 --> 00:02:57.340
arguing they don't want to put out fires,
and warns Esmé about the Woman With Hair

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.980
But No Beard and the Man With A Beard But
No Hair.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:04.350
But Esmé insists on keeping the sugar bowl
herself, as it completes her tea set.

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.310
So, Beatrice and Lemony conspire to steal
it.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:10.860
In their words, "it's a wicked thing to do
for a noble reason."

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:15.030
That phrase, in fact, is a recurrent theme
throughout the final season as the lines between

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:19.730
right and wrong become blurred and the Baudelaires
themselves question whether, at times, they

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:21.180
really acted in a moral way.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:25.959
In fact, much of this season deals with the
idea of moral ambiguity or moral relativism.

00:03:25.959 --> 00:03:31.220
"Is a personal philosophy of moral relativism
the only way to survive in an ethically complex

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:34.260
world, or is it an excuse we use to justify
doing bad things?"

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:39.010
Of course, Beatrice and Lemony's heist ends
up causing an incident that leads to a series

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:40.650
of unfortunate events.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:45.190
When Esmé discovers their plot, she faces
off against them, but Olaf's father steps

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:50.189
in the way and ends up dead from a poisoned
dart that Beatrice intended for Esmé.

00:03:50.189 --> 00:03:53.780
Lemony takes the fall for Beatrice because
he's in love with her, and he escapes with

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:58.689
the Sugar Bowl in a taxi, leaving Beatrice
behind, and that's why he's on the lam during

00:03:58.689 --> 00:04:00.159
the series.

00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:04.870
Olaf vows that Beatrice will burn, which gives
us the best evidence yet that he set the fire

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:09.580
that burned down the Baudelaire mansion, killing
their parents, even though he later casts

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:12.739
doubt on that before he dies on the island.

00:04:12.739 --> 00:04:16.900
Lemony's love for Beatrice also explains why
the narrator of the series is so interested

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:21.019
in the fate of her children and why it's a
bittersweet moment for him when he meets his

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:23.389
niece Beatrice in the final scene.

00:04:23.389 --> 00:04:27.820
The last part of Chapter 14, which serves
as an epilogue to the series, takes place

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:32.810
many years later, probably at least a decade
after the events of the island.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:37.650
In the epilogue, Lemony Snicket comes face-to-face
with Beatrice Baudelaire (the Second).

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.761
She's the daughter that his sister Kit gave
birth to on the island and who the Baudelaires

00:04:41.761 --> 00:04:44.390
raised there for a year before setting sail.

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:48.720
The addition of this final chapter is a nice
nod to the final book from the Unfortunate

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:54.070
Events series and, as it's the 14th chapter,
it takes the story beyond the unlucky number

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:59.340
13, suggesting that the Baudelaires' long
streak of bad luck may finally have been broken.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.990
We can see that Lemony's niece Beatrice survived
and made it back to the mainland at some point,

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.710
however, the fact she doesn't appear to be
with the Baudelaires any longer suggests they

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:10.280
were separated some time through the years.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:13.960
In fact, there are some interesting teases
in what the young Beatrice tells us:

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:17.610
"Female Finnish pirates"
"It was after we sailed away from the island

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:21.310
but before their third trip to Briny Beach,
Violet had just tied up her hair to invent

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:24.240
the steering mechanism and Klaus was studying
tidal charts."

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.690
The "female Finnish pirates" that Beatrice
mentions suggest that after leaving the island

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:32.229
on the boat, the children encountered some
seaborne bandits.

00:05:32.229 --> 00:05:36.110
And the mention is also a nice easter egg
to the knot that Violet uses in the second

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:37.580
episode of season 2:

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:38.760
"This knot's called the Devil's Tongue."

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:41.300
"It was invented by female Finnish pirates
in the 15th century."

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:45.360
The children evidently survived the pirate
encounter because Beatrice says that that

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.660
happened before "their third trip to Briny
Beach".

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.700
That's an interesting detail because in the
original book, Chapter 14 leaves the survival

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:57.440
of the Baudelaires unclear, but this implies
that they did make it back to the mainland

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:02.050
because in the TV series we only see the Baudelaires
visit that beach twice.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:06.320
Once in the very first episode where they
receive the terrible news from Mr Poe that

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.490
their parents have died in a fire.

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:12.509
And a second time in the fourth episode of
season 3 when the Baudelaires land in the

00:06:12.509 --> 00:06:16.639
Queequeg on Briny Beach where they meet Mr
Poe again, although they ignore him and leave

00:06:16.639 --> 00:06:18.349
in Kit's taxi.

00:06:18.349 --> 00:06:22.199
I'd like to think that the Baudelaires did
made it back alive, even if they did have

00:06:22.199 --> 00:06:25.130
to contend with a set of pirates on the way.

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:29.479
And just maybe, given that Violet learns to
tie a knot from them, those Finnish pirates

00:06:29.479 --> 00:06:32.729
actually ended up helping the Baudelaires
on their way back home.

00:06:32.729 --> 00:06:37.610
By the way, when the camera pulls back from
the diner for the final shot of the city,

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:42.539
notice the ever-present VFD symbol in the
pattern of the buildings and streets, a little

00:06:42.539 --> 00:06:46.050
hint perhaps that the VFD organisation lives
on.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:51.440
Although Count Olaf really was absolutely
awful to the poor Baudelaires, his deliciously

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:56.360
wicked and hilariously stupid ways certainly
made him entertaining to watch and he was

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:58.879
brilliantly played by Neil Patrick Harris
.

00:06:58.879 --> 00:07:04.669
Just like Kit Snicket, Olaf doesn't make it
out of the series alive, however, unlike Kit,

00:07:04.669 --> 00:07:09.669
in the end, Olaf died not from the Medusoid
Mycelium, but from the harpoon that Ishmael

00:07:09.669 --> 00:07:10.949
shot into his stomach.

00:07:10.949 --> 00:07:16.160
A very fascinating detail in both the opera
flashback and the scene on the island is that

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:18.860
Olaf was actually in love with Kit Snicket.

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:23.379
This confirms a suspicion a lot of book readers
had and it also explains some of his indifference

00:07:23.379 --> 00:07:27.539
towards his girlfriend Esmé and why he didn't
seem that bothered about breaking up with

00:07:27.539 --> 00:07:28.539
her.

00:07:28.539 --> 00:07:32.740
Remember, the show did hint at Olaf's feelings
for Kit previously, for example, in Season

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:36.060
2 via the names carved inside a heart.

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:40.370
The other big reveal in the final episode
is that Ishmael is actually the original founder

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:41.440
of VFD.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.970
He started the secret organisation when he
was principal of Prufrock Prep after he spotted

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:48.800
talented pupils with what he calls "a gleam
in their eyes".

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:54.240
For Ishmael, the idea behind VFD was to fight
figurative, not literal, fires, though like

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.771
the constantly referenced novel Moby-Dick
where the whale is both a literal whale and

00:07:58.771 --> 00:08:04.500
a metaphor for death, the fires that the VFD
ends up involved in turn out to be both literal

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:05.500
and figurative.

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:09.970
Ishmael says he wanted the gifted students
inside VFD to "come together to stand against

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:15.139
the injustice of this world" to make it "a
quieter, safer place", which fits in with

00:08:15.139 --> 00:08:19.379
the motto we saw at VFD Headquarters: "The
world is quiet here".

00:08:19.379 --> 00:08:24.080
The problem was that the organisation became
divided and split apart in the schism, so

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:27.819
Ish gave up on the world and went to live
in exile on the island.

00:08:27.819 --> 00:08:31.919
The supposed paradise that Ishmael established
is like the Garden of Eden.

00:08:31.919 --> 00:08:36.350
There's even a tree with special apples on
the other side of the island, a clear allusion

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:40.440
to the Tree of Knowledge and its forbidden
fruit, and a snake also offers an apple to

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:43.130
the Baudelaires, just like Adam and Eve.

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:47.780
Ishmael's appearance also reflects a traditional
ideas of God as a bearded man with long flowing

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:50.820
robes who shields his people from potential
harm.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:56.300
Ishmael's people all have to eat plain food
with no spices or flavours and when the Baudelaires

00:08:56.300 --> 00:09:00.370
discover Ish has been putting drugs in the
coconut cordial, he says "there's nothing

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:05.920
wrong with a little opiate for the people",
echoing how Marx dubbed religion as the "opium

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:06.960
of the people".

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:11.390
A central theme of the show and books is that
many people, especially adults, refuse to

00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:16.940
question authority, even in ludicrous situations,
like wearing blindfolds during the trial or

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:20.620
failing to recognise Count Olaf in his crazy
disguises.

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:25.310
Smart characters like the Baudelaires, who
read, educate themselves, question and search

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:29.380
for the truth, are contrasted with foolish
characters like Mr Poe and many others who

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:32.380
refuse to see the reality of what's going
on around them.

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:37.230
The story plays around with the idea of ignorance
as bliss and does so in many ways from the

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:41.960
theme tune's refrain of "look away" to Lemony
Snicket's continued insistence that we stop

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:45.050
watching this dreadful story and do something
more pleasant instead.

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:49.590
It's a clever metaphor for whether it's better
to remain in a state of blissful ignorance

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:52.230
versus the pursuit of knowledge and advancement.

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:56.720
Ish always recommends the islanders discard
anything that washes up on the island for

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:01.230
fear that the object or the technology and
knowledge that it brings might taint the innocence

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:05.600
of the world he's created, in other words,
cause people to question his authority and

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:06.660
create division.

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:11.430
However, he's always keen to point out that
he never forces anyone to do anything, a nod

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:16.700
to mankind's free will, yet the islanders
never do go to the other end of the island

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:20.220
where Ish has been hoarding all the objects
for his own benefit.

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:24.430
Ishmael also knows about the special properties
of the apples in the arboretum and has been

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:25.430
eating them.

00:10:25.430 --> 00:10:29.440
Look carefully when the Medusoid Mycelium
is released and you'll notice that Ish is

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:33.030
the only person who doesn't start coughing
and choking.

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:37.380
Even though Ishmael knows there's a cure in
the arboretum, like many religious authorities,

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:41.890
he demands blind faith from the islanders
as he takes them off on an impossible journey

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:44.880
to reach the horseradish factory on Lousy
Lane.

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:49.720
Fortunately for them, the Incredibly Deadly
Viper swims out to their boat with an apple,

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:53.310
suggesting they could have survived and prevented
the disease from spreading to the mainland.

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:57.640
There's so many references packed into this
final episode and the series as a whole that

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:01.390
I'm working on another video about the show
and how it relates to the books.

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:05.190
I'll add a card with the link here and in
the video description as soon as it's ready.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:09.290
The finale also delivers a few more answers
about the fates of other notable characters

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:10.350
in the series.

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:15.180
The Quagmire triplets' survival is uncertain
in the books, but Chapter 14 of the TV show

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:19.360
tells us that they survived fire and eagles
to be reunited safely.

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:23.600
Fiona's fate in the books was also unclear,
but in the show we see that both she and her

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:32.740
brother Fernald made it through, and end up
reunited with their stepfather Captain Widdershins.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:46.940
Olaf's troupe, who abandon him after the White-Faced
Women refuse to throw Sunny off Mount Fraught,

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:51.580
also get a pleasant ending in the TV series
as we see they finally get to perform on stage

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:53.740
together "at least for one night".

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:58.310
Now that A Series of Unfortunate Events is
over, how do you feel about how it ended?

00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:01.150
And did the show leave any questions unanswered
for you?

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:04.600
Let me know your thoughts and any theories
in the comments below.

